      "writtenOn": "2023-06-21T20:11:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This looks suspicious. We intend `mCvMutex` for protecting the condition variable. However, it seems that we also use it for serializing access to `mState`. If that\u0027s the case, `mState` must be annotated as `GUARDED_BY(mCvMutex)`, and the mutex probably renamed to something more generic like `mLock`. I suspect, access to `mState` must be serialized as `condWaitState` is checking it.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 245,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 245,
        "endChar": 49
      },
      "revId": "63ab1465f8c6b6b4d727a50b167d2dc534b30af4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "66039467_965f4607",
        "filename": "audio/aidl/default/bluetooth/DevicePortProxy.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 25
      },
      "lineNbr": 245,
      "author": {
        "id": 1150374
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-21T23:47:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "not all access to mState is under mutex lock. For instance getState() is used for logging and does not use lock. Only Callbacks controlResultHandler, sessionChangeHandler, condWaitState are locking and updating the state, others are updating with out lock.",
      "parentUuid": "45b46475_3fdee46d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 245,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 245,
        "endChar": 49
      },
      "revId": "63ab1465f8c6b6b4d727a50b167d2dc534b30af4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4d2ff9dd_522a64e9",
        "filename": "audio/aidl/default/bluetooth/DevicePortProxy.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 25
      },
      "lineNbr": 245,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042638
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-22T00:16:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In other words, `mState` must normally be accessed under a lock, and `getState` is a documented exceptional case where we don\u0027t care about consistency across threads. In this case, we actually must annotate `mState` as `GUARDED_BY(mLock)` and annotate `getState` with `NO_THREAD_SAFETY_ANALYSIS` (https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ThreadSafetyAnalysis.html#no-thread-safety-analysis).",
      "parentUuid": "66039467_965f4607",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 245,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 245,
        "endChar": 49
      },
      "revId": "63ab1465f8c6b6b4d727a50b167d2dc534b30af4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "15d7ee35_e64d140d",
        "filename": "audio/aidl/default/bluetooth/DevicePortProxy.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 25
      },
      "lineNbr": 245,
      "author": {
        "id": 1150374
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-22T04:18:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Mikhail, access to mState is not guarded by mutex. This is free for modification. The BluetoothAudioSessionControl has callbacks that convey back the state change status. Basing on this status mState is updated. The conditional wait and mutex serve towards a waiting a predefined for the callback to be serviced.\n\nThe only necessary change is \nstd::condition_variable mInternalCv GUARDED_BY(mCvMutex);\n\nmarking this closed as there is an open comment for this.",
      "parentUuid": "4d2ff9dd_522a64e9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 245,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 245,
        "endChar": 49
      },
      "revId": "63ab1465f8c6b6b4d727a50b167d2dc534b30af4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "03c27e27_69906f0d",
        "filename": "audio/aidl/default/bluetooth/DevicePortProxy.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 25
      },
      "lineNbr": 245,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042638
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-22T15:51:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e access to mState is not guarded by mutex\n\nThis isn\u0027t how this looks to me. It follows from the code that `mState` is used to communicate a state change between two threads. It is set in this method (`sessionChangedHandler`) and then read back in `condWaitState` by another thread. This means, `mState` is a variable shared between two threads. If access to it is not protected by a mutex, then another thread may simply not see the change update because it\u0027s the locking/unlocking that actually ensures the memory state synchronization between different CPU cores. This code uses the canonical protocol described here: http://go/cppref/cpp/thread/condition_variable. Thus, `mState` must be marked as protected by the mutex.\n\nThere is also a comment at the beginning of `DevicePortProxy.h` stating:\n\n```\n * Note: currently, in StreamBluetooth.cpp, if getState() is only used for\n * verbose logging, it is not locked, so the state may not be synchronized.\n```\n\nThis implies that the legacy code assumes that normally, access to `mState` must be locked and `getState` is an exception where we don\u0027t care about the consistency because it\u0027s only used for verbose logging.",
      "parentUuid": "15d7ee35_e64d140d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 245,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 245,
        "endChar": 49
      },
      "revId": "63ab1465f8c6b6b4d727a50b167d2dc534b30af4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fc5795a6_93bd4e8e",
        "filename": "audio/aidl/default/bluetooth/DevicePortProxy.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 25
      },
      "lineNbr": 245,
      "author": {
        "id": 1150374
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-22T19:46:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "mikhail, every access to mState is not locked. Not only getState, setState modifies mState with out lock. Further start(), suspend() sets mState to transient state STARTING, SUSPENDING with out lock. stop() modifies mState with out lock as well. Logging function read mState as well with out lock.\n\nA call to start() or suspend() triggers a callback. This callback returns a status. If this status is saved inside the class, the condWaitState, after the callback is serviced and can use the status to update mState. This way to update mState we never have to lock. The mutex and conditional pair is used to determine if the callbacks are serviced. Updating mState is a side effect of the callback. The primary purpose of mutex and cv are checking if status is received.\n\nThe current implementation has moved the mState setting to callback from condWaitState instead of preserving return status. mState need not be guarded by mutex. The mutex and callback goal is to check if callback is called.",
      "parentUuid": "03c27e27_69906f0d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 245,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 245,
        "endChar": 49
      },
      "revId": "63ab1465f8c6b6b4d727a50b167d2dc534b30af4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
